      summary: "Wells Fargo's data center tracker, compiled by analyst Shahriar Pourreza, counted 374 active local moratoria on data center development by the end of summer 2026, up from 92 in June — a roughly fourfold increase in one quarter — with Michigan, Ohio, North Carolina, Iowa, and Tennessee leading in count. Active moratoria now average roughly 346 days, and the average across all tracked measures rose to 311 days from 277, with some running as long as five years. A separate state-level tracker counted 26 of 50 states restricting or considering restrictions as of September 7, including 18 with active bans or moratoria. New York moved first statewide: after the Legislature passed the Responsible Data Center Development Act on June 4, 2026, Governor Hochul declined to sign it and instead issued Executive Order No. 62 on July 14, pausing discretionary environmental permitting for data centers above 50 megawatts for one year; Seattle adopted an emergency municipal siting moratorium on June 9.",
      significance: "Developers have begun challenging these pauses rather than waiting them out, and early results suggest hastily adopted moratoria are legally vulnerable — RCM Hill, LLC's $100M federal suit over Hill County, Texas's moratorium (arguing Texas counties lack zoning authority) prompted rescission within two weeks; ECO TIP West LLC is suing Chatham County, North Carolina, claiming $11M in reliance spending on a by-right permit created statutory vested rights under N.C. Gen. Stat. § 160D-107; and a California developer is challenging Imperial County's emergency interim ordinance for failing the 'current and immediate threat' showing required by Cal. Gov't Code § 65858(c). The dispute mix this pattern produces — ultra vires challenges, vested-rights and estoppel claims, procedural attacks on emergency findings, and eventually regulatory takings, contract-impairment, dormant commerce clause, and Federal Power Act preemption theories — should concern any owner, developer, or investor holding land optioned or contracted for data center use, since the practical exposure is a stalled entitlement with carrying costs and no clear off-ramp. Because most moratoria are transitional, the setback, water-use, noise, and community-benefit standards adopted during the pause matter more for long-run project economics than the pause itself.",
