      sourceUrl: "https://commercialobserver.com/2026/09/cre-clo-distress-cred-iq/",
      summary: "CRED iQ data published September 8, 2026 shows the CRE CLO distress rate rising from 19% in July to 28% in August, the sharpest one-month move of any deal type this year, while single-asset, single-borrower (SASB) CMBS distress has held near 22% since June; conduit, Freddie Mac and single-family rental categories all remain under 5%. The distress is concentrated in 2021–2022 vintages: FSRIA 2021-FL3 alone has $353M of multifamily collateral in special servicing across seven loans, adding River Crossing at Roswell ($49M) and Grace Abernathy Apartments in Sandy Springs ($42M) in August, and ARCLO 2022-FL1 added the Residences at Medical in San Antonio ($27M) and Pebblebrook Apartments in Redlands ($12M). Five deals account for 38% of CRE CLO special-servicing balance, and Texas, Florida and Georgia hold 44% of the distressed balance. On the SASB side, four office and lab deals — BXHPP 2021-FILM ($525M, Hollywood studios/offices), ALEN 2021-ACEN ($203M, Three Allen Center, Houston), LIFE 2021-BMR ($190M, life sciences) and BSREP 2021-DC ($162M, eight D.C.-area office buildings, transferred Aug. 10) — make up 64% of the category's $1.7B distressed balance.",
      significance: "CRE CLO collateral is short-term floating-rate bridge debt with interest-rate-cap requirements, debt-yield or DSCR extension tests, and holdback-funded business plans, so maturity failures tend to produce disputes over whether extension conditions were met and future advances wrongly withheld, fast-track mezzanine UCC foreclosures met by commercial-reasonableness challenges, and claims against guarantors under completion, carry and carve-out guaranties. Serial defaults inside a single CLO invite noteholder scrutiny of underwriting and collateral management, and in syndicated multifamily deals passive investors often turn on sponsors once equity is wiped out. With office and mixed-use loans maturing over the next nine months pricing 170–180 bps above their in-place notes, as Commercial Observer reports, SASB borrowers face servicer fights over appraisal reductions, modifications and sale-versus-foreclosure decisions.",
